    {
      "ordinal": 17,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "7.1",
      "title": "Historical Sources and Datation",
      "body": "A gorget was a type of Neck protection worn over the neck, clavicles, and shoulders. It evolved from earlier forms of neck defenses, which often relied on chainmail or padded fabrics like the Aventail.\n\nBy the late 15th century, as plate armor became more refined, the gorget emerged as a key piece of protection for the throat and neck, made primarily from steel plates. Gorgets were most common in the 16th century but evidence exists of gorgets and plate collars.\n\nThey are Approved for 15th century armors.\n\nThey are Denied for 14th century and Transitional armors.\n\nWade Allen collection - 1510 - Italy",
      "page_from": 9,
      "page_to": 9,
      "stable_key": "neck-protection:7.1",
      "parent_key": "neck-protection:7",
      "review_status": "extracted",
      "review_note": null,
      "topics": [],
      "keywords": [],
      "cross_refs": [],
      "content_sha256": "6565599816ecfde649ee524035c170f389e6758b40d94f9bc96e8a4888b769cf",
      "has_figures": true,
      "extraction": "ocr",
